        You will be given the root cause of a bug, the commit message and the corresponding patch.
        You need to extract all code statements that lead to the bug from the patch, based on the root cause and the commit message.

        Root cause:
        The root cause of the bug is the allocation of memory for the `buffer` using `kmalloc()`, which does not initialize the allocated memory. This can lead to the buffer containing residual data from previous allocations, potentially exposing sensitive information when the memory is accessed and sent to USB devices. Consequently, this can result in an information leak of uninitialized kernel memory.

        Commit message:
        can: peak_usb: pcan_usb_pro: Fix info-leaks to USB devices

Uninitialized Kernel memory can leak to USB devices.

Fix by using kzalloc() instead of kmalloc() on the affected buffers.

Signed-off-by: Tomas Bortoli <tomasbortoli@gmail.com>
Reported-by: syzbot+d6a5a1a3657b596ef132@syzkaller.appspotmail.com
Fixes: f14e22435a27 ("net: can: peak_usb: Do not do dma on the stack")
Cc: linux-stable <stable@vger.kernel.org>
Signed-off-by: Marc Kleine-Budde <mkl@pengutronix.de>  

        Patch:

 static int pcan_usb_pro_drv_loaded(struct peak_usb_device *dev, int loaded)
 {
 	u8 *buffer;
 	int err;

-	buffer = kmalloc(PCAN_USBPRO_FCT_DRVLD_REQ_LEN, GFP_KERNEL);
+	buffer = kzalloc(PCAN_USBPRO_FCT_DRVLD_REQ_LEN, GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!buffer)
 		return -ENOMEM;

 	buffer[0] = 0;
 	buffer[1] = !!loaded;

 	err = pcan_usb_pro_send_req(dev, PCAN_USBPRO_REQ_FCT,
 				    PCAN_USBPRO_FCT_DRVLD, buffer,
 				    PCAN_USBPRO_FCT_DRVLD_REQ_LEN);
 	kfree(buffer);

 	return err;
 }



        Provide a response strictly in the following format:
        Code statements:
        <text1>
        <text2>
        ...
        <textN>

Code statements:
```c
-	buffer = kmalloc(PCAN_USBPRO_FCT_DRVLD_REQ_LEN, GFP_KERNEL);
+	buffer = kzalloc(PCAN_USBPRO_FCT_DRVLD_REQ_LEN, GFP_KERNEL);
```
